{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 1659 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "5a7cebdd5bcc7078f8b91c48c9848e06480c6fd3fe60d035e2d6a16a23e85099" :edb/db-before-t 1658 :edb/db-after-t 1659 :edb/tx-data [[13194139534971 50 #inst "2026-10-08T12:40:38.254Z" 13194139534971 true] [17592186133500 1100 "If ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/16e82b08842bdec1de657e14110b08037cca4452d1f0e8c64a8fd783037adeb6.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/559785dc3071ba5018f9b97d45e18e9f7bf937f702f9eeaf0a9c5ae087d5b43f.png) are orthogonal vectors, find the value of $k.$" 13194139534971 true] [17592186133500 1100 "If $\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ k \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ 6 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 8\\end{bmatrix}$ are orthogonal vectors, find the value of $k$." 13194139534971 false] [17592186133500 1145 "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot v &= u_{1} \\cdot v_{1} + u_{2} \\cdot v_{2} + u_{3} \\cdot v_{3} + u_{4} \\cdot v_{4} \\\\\n&= 5 \\cdot (-4) + (-3) \\cdot 6 + k \\cdot (-6) + (-2) \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&=-20 - 18 - 6k - 16 \\\\\n&=-6k - 54\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the last line to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-6k - 54 &= 0 \\\\\n- 6k &= 54 \\\\\nk &= -9\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534971 false] [17592186133500 1145 "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-4)+(-3)\\cdot 6+k\\cdot (-6)+(-2)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-20-18-6k-16 \\\\  & =-6k-54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the last line to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6k-54 & =0 \\\\ -6k & =54 \\\\ k & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534971 true] [17592186133501 1100 "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/61cb89adc9a0cce6a434c41be5d2d1799e01e94bebe1b4f44d48bc011c1a349b.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/00af13e5017635a0b333ecd5fc2bfc761c9612427b8a3986ba48fa904ff2eed0.png) be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u} \\!\\perp\\! \\mathbf{w}$. Find $(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})\\cdot \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{u}\\cdot(\\mathbf{v}+ \\mathbf{w}).$" 13194139534971 true] [17592186133501 1100 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u} \\!\\perp\\! \\mathbf{w}$. Find $(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})\\cdot \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{u}\\cdot(\\mathbf{v}+ \\mathbf{w})$." 13194139534971 false] [17592186133501 1145 "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}=0\n$$\n\n. Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n(u + v) \\cdot w + u \\cdot (v + w) &= u \\cdot w + v \\cdot w + u \\cdot v + u \\cdot w \\\\\n&= 0 + v \\cdot w + u \\cdot v + 0 \\\\\n&= v \\cdot w + u \\cdot v \\\\\n&= (2 \\cdot (-1) + (-3) \\cdot 3 + 4 \\cdot 1) \\\\\n&= + ((-1) \\cdot 2 + (-2) \\cdot (-3) + 5 \\cdot 4) \\\\\n&= (-2 - 9 + 4) + (-2 + 6 + 20) \\\\\n&=-7 + 24 \\\\\n&= 17\n\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534971 false] [17592186133501 1145 "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}=0$. Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u+v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+w) & =u\\cdot w+v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+u\\cdot w \\\\  & =0+v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+0 \\\\  & =v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 1) \\\\  & +((-1)\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot 4)\\, \\\\  & =(-2-9+4)+(-2+6+20) \\\\  & =-7+24 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534971 true] [17592186133502 1100 "Let ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/618da6573504088a90064c503b3603f9c1cbedaee493473db435765ca635bdb9.png) and ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/a71f47b53dab435e794fa5c4c889141b8ce32c93715abd56e0dc48d6535865b8.png) be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$. What is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139534971 true] [17592186133502 1100 "Let $\\begin{bmatrix}-8 \\\\ a \\\\ b \\\\ -10\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}$ be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$. What is the value of $a+b$?" 13194139534971 false] [17592186133502 1145 "Given that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}=0$. Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ -8\\cdot 6+a\\cdot (-15)+b\\cdot 9+(-10)\\cdot (-12) & =0 \\\\ -48-15a+9b+120 & =0 \\\\ -15a+9b & =-72 \\\\ -5a+3b & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ -8\\cdot 4+a\\cdot 2+b\\cdot (-5)+(-10)\\cdot (-3) & =0 \\\\ -32+2a-5b+30 & =0 \\\\ 2a-5b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}-5a+3b=-24 \\\\ 2a-5b=2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $5$, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-10a+6b=-48 \\\\ 10a-25b=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, adding the above equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n-19b = -38 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad b = 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b = 2$ into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-5(2) & =2 \\\\ 2a-10 & =2 \\\\ 2a & =12 \\\\ a & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b = 6 + 2 = 8.\n$$" 13194139534971 true] [17592186133502 1145 "Given that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$, we have\n\n$$\n{u \\cdot v = 0; u \\cdot w = 0\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}=0\n$$\n\n. Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot v &= 0 \\\\\n- 8 \\cdot 6 + a \\cdot (-15) + b \\cdot 9 + (-10) \\cdot (-12) &= 0 \\\\\n- 48 - 15a + 9b + 120 &= 0 \\\\\n- 15a + 9b &= -72 \\\\\n- 5a + 3b &= -24\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}=0\n$$\n\n, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot w &= 0 \\\\\n- 8 \\cdot 4 + a \\cdot 2 + b \\cdot (-5) + (-10) \\cdot (-3) &= 0 \\\\\n- 32 + 2a - 5b + 30 &= 0 \\\\\n2a - 5b &= 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n{-5a + 3b =-24; 2a - 5b = 2\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $5$, we get\n\n$$\n{-10a + 6b =-48; 10a - 25b = 10\n$$\n\nNow, adding the above equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n-19b = -38 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad b = 2\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b = 2$ into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2a - 5(2) &= 2 \\\\\n2a - 10 &= 2 \\\\\n2a &= 12 \\\\\na &= 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b = 6 + 2 = 8\n$$" 13194139534971 false] [17592186133553 1100 "Given the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/b8e1e42d3c85f7b3783a0c46d5075e0c588f22a030f3d6a84da9ad3444ad35d0.png), which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $\\mathbf{u}$?\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14044739/assets/6e69c8002a518487ce4821efe1faae85ba7ec365b1bedf23d7bfcd30e10e42b5.png)" 13194139534971 true] [17592186133553 1100 "Given the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$, which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $\\mathbf{u}$?\n$\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139534971 false] [17592186133553 1145 "We compute the dot product between $\\mathbf{u}$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot v &= u_{1} \\cdot v_{1} + u_{2} \\cdot v_{2} + u_{3} \\cdot v_{3} + u_{4} \\cdot v_{4} \\\\\n&= (-5) \\cdot 1 + (-3) \\cdot (-3) + 2 \\cdot (-4) + 0 \\cdot 9 \\\\\n&=-5 + 9 - 8 + 0 \\\\\n&=-4.\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot w &= u_{1} \\cdot w_{1} + u_{2} \\cdot w_{2} + u_{3} \\cdot w_{3} + u_{4} \\cdot w_{4} \\\\\n&= (-5) \\cdot 2 + (-3) \\cdot 2 + 2 \\cdot 8 + 0 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n&=-10 - 6 + 16 + 0 \\\\\n&= 0.✓\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nu \\cdot x &= u_{1} \\cdot x_{1} + u_{2} \\cdot x_{2} + u_{3} \\cdot x_{3} + u_{4} \\cdot x_{4} \\\\\n&= (-5) \\cdot 2 + (-3) \\cdot (-4) + 2 \\cdot (-8) + 0 \\cdot 6 \\\\\n&=-10 + 12 - 16 + 0 \\\\\n&=-14.\\cdot\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $\\mathbf{w}$ only." 13194139534971 false] [17592186133553 1145 "We compute the dot product between $\\mathbf{u}$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-5+9-8+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 8+0\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-10-6+16+0 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot x & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {x}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot (-8)+0\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-10+12-16+0 \\\\  & =-14.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $\\mathbf{w}$ only." 13194139534971 true] [17592186133693 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323628 13194139534971 true] [17592186133693 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323649 13194139534971 true] [17592186133693 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323663 13194139534971 true] [17592186133693 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323705 13194139534971 true] [17592186133693 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323712 13194139534971 true] [17592186133694 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323635 13194139534971 true] [17592186133694 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323642 13194139534971 true] [17592186133694 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323719 13194139534971 true] [17592186133694 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323726 13194139534971 true] [17592186133695 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323677 13194139534971 true] [17592186133695 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323733 13194139534971 true] [17592186133695 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323740 13194139534971 true] [17592186133738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323656 13194139534971 true] [17592186133738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323670 13194139534971 true] [17592186133738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323684 13194139534971 true] [17592186133738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323691 13194139534971 true] [17592186133738 1020 #edb/ref 17592186323698 13194139534971 true] [17592186134134 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534971 true] [17592186134134 1100 "Let $u = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $w = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix}$ be vectors such that $u⊥v$. Find $(v + w) \\cdot u + v \\cdot (u + w)$." 13194139534971 false] [17592186134134 1100 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,v.$ Find $(v+w)\\cdot u+v\\cdot (u+w).$" 13194139534971 true] [17592186134134 1145 "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot v=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(v+w)\\cdot u+v\\cdot (u+w) & =v\\cdot u+w\\cdot u+v\\cdot u+v\\cdot w \\\\  & =0+w\\cdot u+0+v\\cdot w \\\\  & =w\\cdot u+v\\cdot w \\\\  & =((-3)\\cdot 3+2\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot 1+(-2)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & +(2\\cdot (-3)+4\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 5+(-1)\\cdot (-2)) \\\\  & =(-9-4+5+4)+(-6+8+0+2) \\\\  & =-4+4 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534971 true] [17592186134136 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534971 true] [17592186134136 1100 "Let $u = \\begin{bmatrix}a \\\\ 3 \\\\ b \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$, and $w = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$. What is the value of $a + b$?" 13194139534971 false] [17592186134136 1100 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ 3 \\\\ b \\\\ -5\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139534971 true] [17592186134136 1145 "Given that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot 2+3\\cdot (-5)+b\\cdot 3+(-5)\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ 2a-15+3b+20 & =0 \\\\ 2a+3b & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ a\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot (-2)+b\\cdot 5+(-5)\\cdot 3 & =0 \\\\ -4a-6+5b-15 & =0 \\\\ -4a+5b & =21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2a+3b=-5 \\\\ -4a+5b=21.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n11b=11\\,⟹\\,b=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=1$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a+3(1) & =-5 \\\\ 2a+3 & =-5 \\\\ 2a & =-8 \\\\ a & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b=-4+1=-3.\n$$" 13194139534971 true] [17592186134177 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139534971 true] [17592186134177 1100 "Let $u = \\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ a \\\\ b \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}, v = \\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$, and $w = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}$ be vectors such that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w$. What is the value of $a + b$?" 13194139534971 false] [17592186134177 1100 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ a \\\\ b \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" 13194139534971 true] [17592186134177 1145 "Given that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w$, we have\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ -3\\cdot 3+a\\cdot 4+b\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ -9+4a+b-4 & =0 \\\\ 4a+b & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ -3\\cdot 1+a\\cdot 1+b\\cdot (-3)-4\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ -3+a-3b+16 & =0 \\\\ a-3b & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}4a+b=13 \\\\ a-3b=-13\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13a=26\\,⟹\\,a=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2)+b & =13 \\\\ 8+b & =13 \\\\ b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b=2+5=7.\n$$" 13194139534971 true] [17592186323628 1017 #edb/ref 17592186323629 13194139534971 true] [17592186323628 1089 "q-140123" 13194139534971 true] [17592186323628 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139534971 true] [17592186323628 1100 "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ 1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}3k \\\\ -6 \\\\ k \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that  $u⊥v.$ Find the value of $k.$" 13194139534971 true] [17592186323628 1113 #uuid "6462a8a7-0592-5fe5-81e2-36fc28907977" 13194139534971 true] [17592186323628 1145 "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =k\\cdot 3k+1\\cdot (-6)+3\\cdot k+(-2)\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =3{k}^{2}-6+3k+0 \\\\  & =3({k}^{2}+k-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({k}^{2}+k-2) & =0 \\\\ 3(k+2)(k-1) & =0 \\\\ k & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534971 true] [17592186323629 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323630 13194139534971 true] [17592186323629 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323631 13194139534971 true] [17592186323629 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323632 13194139534971 true] [17592186323629 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323633 13194139534971 true] [17592186323629 1000 #edb/ref 17592186323634 13194139534971 true] [17592186323629 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139534971 true] [17592186323629 1041 "selection" 13194139534971 true] [17592186323629 1104 #edb/ref 17592186323632 13194139534971 true] [17592186323629 1110 #uuid "098f2ecc-6d21-5a9e-9d6c-5236648f2c40" 13194139534971 true] [17592186323630 10